    "prompt": "Function `HttpParser.parse_headers` in aiohttp/http_parser.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\ndef parse_headers(\n        self, lines: List[bytes]\n    ) -> Tuple[\n        \"CIMultiDictProxy[str]\", RawHeaders, Optional[bool], Optional[str], bool, bool\n    ]:\n        headers, raw_headers = self._headers_parser.parse_headers(lines)\n        close_conn = None\n        encoding = None\n        upgrade = False\n        chunked = False\n\n        # https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-5.5-6\n        # https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-collected-abnf\n        singletons = (\n            hdrs.CONTENT_LENGTH,\n            hdrs.CONTENT_LOCATION,\n            hdrs.CONTENT_RANGE,\n            hdrs.CONTENT_TYPE,\n            hdrs.ETAG,\n            hdrs.HOST,\n            hdrs.MAX_FORWARDS,\n            hdrs.SERVER,\n            hdrs.TRANSFER_ENCODING,\n            hdrs.USER_AGENT,\n        )\n        bad_hdr = next((h for h in singletons if len(headers.getall(h, ())) > 1), None)\n        if bad_hdr is not None:\n            raise BadHttpMessage(f\"Duplicate '{bad_hdr}' header found.\")\n\n        # keep-alive\n        conn = headers.get(hdrs.CONNECTION)\n        if conn:\n            v = conn.lower()\n            if v == \"close\":\n                close_conn = True\n            elif v == \"keep-alive\":\n                close_conn = False\n            # https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-101-switching-protocols\n            elif v == \"upgrade\" and headers.get(hdrs.UPGRADE):\n                upgrade = True\n\n        # encoding\n        enc = headers.get(hdrs.CONTENT_ENCODING)\n        if enc:\n            enc = enc.lower()\n            if enc in (\"gzip\", \"deflate\", \"br\"):\n                encoding = enc\n\n        # chunking\n        te = headers.get(hdrs.TRANSFER_ENCODING)\n        if te is not None:\n            if self._is_chunked_te(te):\n                chunked = True\n\n            if hdrs.CONTENT_LENGTH in headers:\n                raise BadHttpMessage(\n                    \"Transfer-Encoding can't be present with Content-Length\",\n                )\n\n        return (headers, raw_headers, close_conn, encoding, upgrade, chunked)\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: parse_headers(self, lines) -> Tuple[\n        \"CIMultiDictProxy[str]\", RawHeaders, Optional[bool], Optional[str], bool, bool\n    ]\ncalls: BadHttpMessage, conn.lower, enc.lower, headers.get, headers.getall, len, next, self._headers_parser.parse_headers, self._is_chunked_te\nreturn statements: return (headers, raw_headers, close_conn, encoding, upgrade, chunked)\nraise statements: raise BadHttpMessage(f\"Duplicate '{bad_hdr}' header found.\") | raise BadHttpMessage(\n                    \"Transfer-Encoding can't be present wi\ncontrol flow: if=8 loops=0 try=0 with=0 returns=1 yields=0 awaits=0\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. `parse_headers` returns a tuple containing various parsed components: a dictionary-like object with header names converted to uppercase, the raw headers as received, flags indicating connection closure, content encoding, protocol upgrade, and chunked transfer encoding status.\nS2. `parse_headers` accepts a list of byte strings (`lines`) representing individual header lines.\nS3. `parse_headers` handles chunked transfer encoding by checking the `Transfer-Encoding` header; if it specifies 'chunked', it sets a flag.\nS4. `parse_headers` is defined in `aiohttp/http_parser.py`.\nS5. `parse_headers` does not have any side effects beyond parsing and returning values.\nS6. `parse_headers` checks for an `Upgrade` header in conjunction with `Connection: upgrade` to indicate protocol switching.\nS7. `parse_headers` examines the `Connection` header to determine whether the connection should be closed or kept alive.\nS8. `parse_headers` parses the `Content-Encoding` header to identify supported encodings like gzip, deflate, or br.\nS9. Header names returned by HttpParser.parse_headers are in upper case.\nS10. HttpParser.parse_headers parses RFC 5322 headers from a stream.\nS11. `parse_headers` checks for duplicate singleton headers (those that should not appear more than once) and raises a `BadHttpMessage` exception if any are found.\nS12. HttpParser.parse_headers returns a list of header name and value pairs.\nS13. `parse_headers` relies on an internal `_headers_parser` method to initially parse the headers into a `CIMultiDictProxy` for easy access and a list of raw headers.\nS14. Line continuations are supported in HttpParser.parse_headers.\nS15. `parse_headers` raises an exception if both `Transfer-Encoding` and `Content-Length` are present, as per RFC 9110.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supported\nS2: contradicted -- <evidence, at most 12 words>\nS3: unresolved",
